#{{ label intro }} Introduction

{{ bible block Mat 24:34-35 }}

The above words of Christ are perhaps some of the most critical found in the scriptures in regards to the integrity
of the New Testament and of Christ's identity. Did or did not all that He said would come to pass *actually* come to
pass within the timeframe He said it would happen. If this would not true, then Christ would then have
prophesied falsely, and our hope in Him would be in vain. What then did He say would come to pass?
The prophecy of Christ in question is the Olivet Discourse regarding the End of the Age,
which is found in {{ bible hover Mat 24 }}, {{ bible hover Luke 21 }}, and {{ bible hover Mark 13 }} of the synoptic
Gospels. There are two distinct parts to Christ's
prophecy in regards to the debate of its fulfillment: the first deals with the judgement against Jerusalem,
and the second half deals with the Coming of the Son of Man at the End of the Age. In this article we will not deal
with the first half of the prophecy, since the exegesis of it in {{ bible hover Luke 21:5-24 }} in combination with
the witness of Josephus {{ cite jewish-war }} clearly marks it's fulfillment in 70 AD with the Siege of Jerusalem.
We will instead deal with primarily the second half, the Coming of the Son of Man. The common understanding of
Eschatology (e.g. the various forms of Premillennialism) largely denies that this had a fulfillment in the 1st century
AD, and thus argue that Christ's reference to the lifespans of the alive generation He was addressing in His day as
the timeframe for His prophecy's fulfillment should *instead* be understood as a *type* of generation who would eventually
experience its fulfillment in the far future relative to Christ's earthly ministry. We contend that this is an
inappropriate departure from the plain meaning of Christ's words mirrored in each of the synoptic Gospel accounts of
the Olivet Discourse, and of the natural use of the word "generation", Γενεὰ (Genea), found elsewhere in the synoptic
Gospels. We instead defend that the End of the Age did in fact occur within the lifespan of those who witnessed Christ's
earthly ministry, specifically with the destruction of the Second Temple in 70 AD. This necessarily means we defend
that the Coming of the Son of Man also coincided with the destruction of the Second Temple, and that the Millennial Reign
of Christ also began at that moment. While this view of the End of the Age is common within mainstream Amillennialism
and Postmillennialism, we also defend the controversial position that the First Resurrection of
{{ bible hover Rev 20:4-6 }} not only is meant to *necessarily* coincide with the Coming of the Son of Man, but that
it *did* occur alongside it in 70 AD. However, we will demonstrate from {{ bible hover Rev 20:4-6 }} that while the
First Resurrection *is* a physical resurrection, it is strictly limited as a reward to the Apostolic Church leading up to
the End of the Age, and that these Apostolic Christians are *currently* ruling and
reigning with Christ *in heaven* with
the same physical immortality that Christ received at His resurrection and *still* possesses *in heaven*. Defending this
understanding of the First Resurrection from {{ bible hover Rev 20:4-6 }} also means we necessarily defend the
Second Resurrection of {{ bible hover Rev 20:11-14 }} as a general physical resurrection of both the enemies and the
Remnant of God from across the ages, including Millennial Christians, marking the
end of history and the Day of Judgement. While it is controversial to argue that the Coming of the Son of Man and the
First Resurrection occurred in
70 AD, we believe this due to the Second Coming of Christ being ill-defined, since the Second Coming is commonly
conflated with the end of history and the Day of Judgement. We instead propose that the Second Coming should be used as
a term to refer exclusively the Coming of the Son of Man at the End of the Age in 70 AD,
and the Second Resurrection as a term should be favoured to refer to the Day of Judgement at the end of the Millennium.
We believe this variant of Postmillennialism, defending the First Resurrection as a physical resurrection occurring
in 70 AD, help's to explain difficult facets of the Epistles in the New Testament that insist upon the imminence of both
the Second Coming *alongside* a physical resurrection of the "dead in Christ"
{{ bible cite 1 Thess 4:14-16, 1 Cor 15:22-27 }}. We also propose that the other difficult facets of the Epistles
that refer to either the Rapture of Christians {{ bible cite 1 Thess 4:17 }} or Christian's who are alive at the Last
Trumpet receiving immortality {{ bible cite 1 Cor 15:51-52 }} should be instead be understood as being a result of the
Apostles conveying *partial* revelation they received regarding the End of the Age and the upcoming Millennium which
should be interpreted in light of the *full* revelation of the End of the Age and the upcoming Millennium as found
in the Book of Revelation. Consequently, we propose that passages that are understood as referring to the Rapture
should instead be interpreted as referring to the *process* of the elect being gathered *during* the Millennium,
and those passages understood as referring to the Last Trumpet should instead be understood as referring to
Second Resurrection. We are proposing this relatively novel variant of Postmillennialism to challenge
the prevailing dominance of Dispensational Premillennialism, now increasingly recognised for its
inconsistent hermeneutics and its ramifications on Practical Theology, as well to counter valid criticisms of
weak-points in mainstream Postmillennialism. As much as it is painted as a speculative field with little
practical application, Eschatology in fact has far-reaching impacts on wider theology. This is most obvious in
Eschatological positions where the End of the Age is still future to us nowadays, which has been the starting of
the erroneous Charismatic and Pentecostal traditions that assert we are currently experiencing the End of the Age
and are consequently receiving the signs, wonders, and Apostolic gifts of the Holy Spirit
that were promised in the Old Testament for the End of the Age. Dispensational Premillennialism can also result in
teachings in the New Testament dealing with the Kingdom of Heaven as not being applicable to the Christian today, but
can only be regarded as a far-off future reality. In contrast, we hope to demonstrate Postmillennialism in general,
including our own variant, is both
able to provide a consistent argument to refute Charismatic and Pentecostal traditions due to the End of the Age
having already been fulfilled, as well as to uphold the New Testament teachings regarding the Kingdom of Heaven
as presently applicable to the current Millennium.

# Defence

##{{ label coming }} The Coming of the Son of Man

{{ bible block Mat 24:29-30 }}

As stated in {{ label intro }}, the starting point of this entire argument is of the Olivet Discourse having
*a* fulfillment in the Siege of Jerusalem and ultimately in the destruction of the Second Temple. Consequently,
the tribulation being referred to in {{ bible hover Mat 24:29 }} should be understood as the great tribulation
against Jerusalem, which is especially clear in Luke's account of the Olivet Discourse. There is a clear chronological
marker made in {{ bible hover Mat 24:29 }} that is placed "immediately after the tribulation of those days", which
would coincide with the destruction of the Second Temple in 70 AD *after* Jerusalem had already been conquered.
The events described of the sun being darkened, the moon not giving its light, and the stars of heaven falling, are
meant to have taken place at this point in time. While it may seem that a literal astronomical event
was required for its fulfillment, the portions of the Old Testament that are being quoted reveal what it actually means.
The are a number of Old Testament passages this verse could be drawing from, with the most direct quotation in verse 29
being found in Isaiah 13:

{{ bible block Isa 13:9-11 }}

Isaiah 13 is an oracle of judgement against Babylon {{ bible cite Isa 13:1 }}, which experienced a fulfillment in the
Medo-Persian conquest of Babylon in the 6th century BC {{ bible cite Isa 13:17-19 }}. It's quotation by Christ in
Matthew 24 however signify's that the judgement against Jerusalem is at least on par with the judgement against
Babylon, and that Jerusalem is being spoken of *as* Babylon. In fact, the second quotation in Matthew 24:29 from
Isaiah 14 reinforces the point that Jerusalem should be regarded as the *greater* Babylon foretold in the Old Testament.
Prior to demonstrating this however, the context of the Isaiah 14 quotation should be established.
The beginning of Isaiah 14 follows on from the end of Isaiah 13 in its description of Israel returning to the land
after the defeat of Babylon at the hands of the Medo-Persians:

{{ bible block Isa 14:1-2 }}

While there was a fulfillment of this in history with the return from the Babylonian Exile, the part of this passage
where the Israelites take their Babylonian captors captive was not fulfilled in this time. However, the passage goes on:

{{ bible block Isa 14:3-11 }}

Up to this point the passage could be understood simply as a supernatural view of the human kings of Babylon being
dragged down into the underworld and being received by other fallen kings. However, verse 12 onwards clarifies the
identity of the *singular* King of Babylon:

{{ bible block Isa 14:12-15 }}

Here the King of Babylon is spoken of in supernatural terms, one who was once in heaven, but after being cast out is seeking
to overthrow the powers of heaven. {{ bible hover Isa 14:16 }} returns to speaking of the King of Babylon in human terms,
but there are examples elsewhere is scripture such as {{ bible hover Eze 28 }} where a human king and a separate
supernatural entity are addressed simultaneously. Due to the parallels between {{ bible hover Isaiah 14:12-15 }} and
{{ bible hover Ezekiel 28:11-19 }}, the Son of the Dawn in Isaiah 14 is most likely referring to Satan, specifically
his pre-Fall identity. The defeat of Babylon, an outpost of Satan's dominion, in history, definitely had a
supernatural aftermath, but just as there was an unfulfilled component of Israel's subjugation of Babylon, there is
also an unfulfilled component of Satan's total humiliation by this point in history. Consequently, with Jerusalem
being identified as the greater Babylon in the Olivet Discourse, the defeat of greater Babylon must also coincide
with the total humiliation of the King of Babylon, Satan. Satan's humiliation in {{ bible hover Isa 14:15 }} involving
being brought down to the deepest parts of Sheol also correlates with Satan being bound and cast into the Abyss at the
beginning of the Millennium in {{ bible hover Rev 20:3 }}, which will be expounded later. In conclusion, Christ in
{{ bible hover Mat 24:29 }} proclaiming that the end of the tribulation of Jerusalem in 70 AD will also result in
the "sun being darkened", the "moon not giving its light", the "stars falling from heaven", should be understood as
Christ equating the throwing down of Jerusalem with the throwing down of
greater Babylon and its king, Satan, on the Day of Yahweh.
The argument for this equation is further bolstered by a study of the Book of
Revelation, which will be left to a later section.

{{ bible block Mat 24:30 }}

The "sign of the Son of Man" at face value could seem to be referring to another physical astronomical event,
but as we have seen in the proceeding verse this would go against the pattern of Christ's and the Old Testament's
use of astronomical language. The only other reference to a "sign of the Son of Man" in either the Old or New
Testaments however is from the beginning of the Olivet Discourse:

{{ bible block Mat 24:1-3 }}

Here the disciples are associating the sign of Christ's coming with not only the End of the Age, but also the
destruction of the Second Temple. Consequently, the destruction of the Second Temple can be understood as an
*earthly* sign of Christ's coming. However, Christ states that the sign of the Son of Man will also appear in *heaven*
(the underlying Greek for "sky" is οὐρανῷ, or ouranō, which can equally be translated as "heaven"). Therefore, if
this is meant to be a *heavenly* sign, then it rules out an physical astronomical sign as being its fulfillment.
Considering the passage where the Coming of the Son of Man is quoted from however, the *sign* being heavenly is
further evident:

{{ bible block Dan 7:13-14 }}

The vision of Daniel is centred around the destruction of the Fourth Beast and the Little Horn, and does not occur
at the end of history. The scene of the Ancient of Days seated on His throne also takes place in heaven, not on
Earth, and the Son of Man receives the everlasting dominion from the Ancient of Days in heaven. Consequently, Christ
quotes this portion of Daniel 7 *not* to claim that He will physically and visibly descend onto the Earth, but that
He will receive the everlasting kingdom from the Ancient of Days at the very end of Jerusalem's tribulation,
the destruction of the Second Temple. When it states in {{ bible hover Mat 24:30 }} that all the tribes of the earth
will see and mourn, it must be noted that the Greek term for "earth", γῆς (gēs), can equivalently be translated as
"land", and in combination with the term "tribe", as well as being in the context of the tribulation of Jerusalem,
it can be understood as a specific reference to the Tribes of Israel
in the Promised Land mourning, *not* all the Nations of the World.

So far, it has been demonstrated that the details of {{ bible hover Mat 24:29-30 }} leading up to the Coming of the Son
of Man and the End of the Age can be understood as having a
supernatural and heavenly fulfillment based off its direct Old Testament quotations, as opposed to being fulfilled
physically or astronomically. {{ bible hover Mat 24:31 }} represents a turning point in Christ's prophetic timeline
however, with it describing events that take place *after* the End of the Age:

{{ bible block Mat 24:31 }}

While this passage is commonly interpreted as referring to the instantaneous Rapture of believers, all that this passage
states is that Christ at the beginning of the Age to Come sends forth His angels for the *purpose* of gathering His
elect from every corner of the Earth. As will be further demonstrated in the section {{ label millennium }},
this Gathering, what is commonly referring to as the Rapture, should be understood as a process that *began* in 70 AD,
and not as an instantaneous event.

##{{ label first-res }} The First Resurrection

{{ bible block Rev 20:4-6 }}

This section will aim to demonstrate that the First Resurrection in {{ bible hover Rev 20:4-6 }} should not only be
understood as having occurred at the End of the Age in 70 AD, but that it should be identified with the repeated
statements found in the New Testament Epistles regarding the expectation of the dead in Christ rising *first* at
His coming.

The context of {{ bible hover Rev 20:4-6 }} must first be noted. The First Resurrection takes place at the beginning
of the Millennium, after Satan has been bound so that he can no longer deceive the nations for the entirety of the
Millennium {{ bible cite Rev 20:2-3 }}.
Those who receive the First Resurrection are specifically those who are Christ's martyrs and had not worshipped
the Beast, it's Image, and had not received it's Mark {{ bible cite Rev 20:4 }}. This subset of humanity are the
only ones who are resurrected, with rest of humanity only resurrecting at the end of the Millennium
{{ bible cite Rev 20:5 }}. Those who receive the First Resurrection will then reign with Christ and be priests before
Him for
the duration of the Millennium. Considering that those who received the First Resurrection are those who refused
to receive the Mark of the Beast, then identifying the Beast can help to identify the recipients of the First
Resurrection.

The Beast referred to in {{ bible hover Rev 20:4 }} is the one who was thrown into the Lake of Fire alongside the
False Prophet in {{ bible hover Rev 19:20 }}, just prior to Satan being bound. We know that this occurs prior to
Satan being bound and the beginning of the Millennium, since Satan is thrown into Lake of Fire at the end of the
Millennium where the Beast and the False Prophet already are {{ bible cite Rev 20:10 }}. Importantly, the Beast and the
False Prophet are thrown into the Lake of Fire *at* the coming of Christ
{{ bible hover Rev 19:19 }}. Considering that we have established in section {{ label coming }}
that the Coming of the Son of Man and the End of the Age took place in 70 AD according to the Olivet Discourse, then
the entities, either natural or supernatural, that the Beast and the False Prophet symbolise must also have existed
up until 70 AD. The Beast and the False Prophet matches a typology found throughout The Book of Revelation and Daniel of
there being a powerful pair of God's enemies that co-jointly wage war on His people up until the Coming of the Son of
Man. Prior to their appearance in Revelation 19, the Beast and the False Prophet are depicted as the Scarlett Beast
carrying the Great Harlot in Revelation 17 and 18, and as the Beast of the Sea and the Beast of the Earth in Revelation 13.
This pairing also appears in the Book of Daniel, where prior to the Coming of the Son of Man the Fourth Beast and the
Little Horn wage war upon the saints in Daniel 7, and in Daniel 2 they are depicted as the Feet of Iron and Clay.
These pairings are no coincidence, and each of their depictions
illuminates different aspects of their relationship with each other.

###{{ label daniel-2 }} Daniel 2

{{ bible block Dan 2:31-35 }}

{{ bible block Dan 2:40-44 }}

In Daniel 2 the Legs of Iron are identified as the Fourth Kingdom, which in turn can be conclusively identified as
the Roman Empire. The Feet of Iron and Clay belong to the Legs of Iron, so they can be interpreted as representing a
phase of the Roman Empire right before the coming of the Kingdom of Heaven (represented by the Stone cut without
hands) that takes away its dominion. Multiple interpretations are given in {{ bible hover Dan 2:41-43 }} about the
meaning of the iron mixed with the clay, with the first being that it represents "a divided kingdom"
{{ bible cite Dan 2:41 }}. The iron represents the power of the Roman Empire, but the clay, being an *earthen* material,
can be understood as representing a power *from the land*, i.e. the Promised Land. This union of iron and clay can
be seen in history, where by the New Testament era Judea was deeply intertwined with the wider Roman Empire,
and its rulers exercised their authority with the permission of Rome. This relationship between Jerusalem and Rome
was always strained, however. {{ bible hover Dan 2:43 }} emphasises the ethnic division within this relation
between the Jews and the Romans by noting how the "seed of men" represented by the iron and the clay not clinging
together, just as iron does not combine with clay, yet it remains dangerous due to the presence of the iron. There
is also a focus on the toes of the feet being partly iron and others being partly clay in {{ bible hover Dan 2:42 }},
although the emphasis of the iron and clay is on their relative strengths.
The number ten represented by the number of toes found across the feet will also appear again later in Daniel 7. The
Stone cut without hands is noted as striking the Feet of Iron and Clay "in the days of those kings"
{{ bible cite Dan 2:44 }}, resulting in the entire statue turning to
dust. The imagery of a stone is often used to refer to the Messiah in the Old and New Testament
{{ bible cite Psa 118:22, Isa 28:16, Eph 2:20 }}, and this passage seems to draw upon that imagery while at the same
time identifying
the Stone as the Kingdom of Heaven {{ bible cite Dan 2:44 }}. These two concepts are not in conflict with one another,
and it will be seen in Daniel that the Coming of the Son of Man should be considered as synonymous with the arrival
of the Kingdom of Heaven. Since the Messiah arrived in days of the Julio-Claudian Caesars and the latter days of
Jerusalem, we can confidently conclude that the Iron and the Clay are a reference to the authorities of Rome and
Jerusalem.

###{{ label daniel-7 }} Daniel 7

{{ bible block Dan 7:7-8 }}

{{ bible block Dan 7:11-12 }}

{{ bible block Dan 7:19-27 }}

The pair representing the great enemies of God before the Coming of the Son of Man in {{ bible hover Dan 7:13-14 }}
are named the Fourth Beast and the Little Horn. Due to the Fourth Beast being identified in {{ bible Dan 7:23 }} as the
same Fourth Kingdom in {{ bible hover Dan 2:40 }}, the Fourth Beast can be confidently identified once more as the
Roman Empire. The Fourth Beast is noted as having ten horns, interpreted as being ten kings,
that will arise from it {{ bible cite Dan 7:24 }}.
The natural interpretation of this is that ten rulers of the Roman Empire will arise before the Coming of the Son of
Man. This accords with history, in that there had been ten Caesars of the Roman Empire that had ruled consecutively by
70 AD: Julius Caesar, Augustus, Tiberias, Caligula, Claudius, Nero, Galba, Otho, Vitellius, and Vespasian.
{{ bible hover Dan 7:24 }} also notes that there is another horn, the Little Horn {{ bible cite Dan 7:8 }}, that is
different from the other horns {{ bible cite Dan 7:24 }}. It is different from the other horns in that it is
larger than the other horns, and that it had eyes and a mouth by which it blasphemed and boasted
{{ bible cite Dan 7:20 }}. The Little Horn also arises after the ten horns {{ bible cite Dan 7:24 }},
not in a *temporal* sense, but in a
*positional* sense (the Aramaic word underlying "after" is ambiguous and can equally be translated as "with", "before",
or "in the midst of"). Since the Little Horn arises out of the Fourth Beast but seemingly takes on a life of its own
and turns on the Fourth Beast by uprooting three of its horns {{ bible cite Dan 7:8 }}, the Little Horn should
be understood as an authority that had its origin within the Fourth Kingdom, but then declared independence, rebelled
against the Fourth Kingdom, but then had only a partial victory against it that it took pride in. The Little Horn is
also noted as attempting to "make changes in seasons and in law"{{ bible cite Dan 7:25 }}. This reference to "law"
should be understood as referring to the Law of Moses, since it is spoken of not in relation to the laws of the
Fourth Kingdom, but instead is spoken of in the context of the Little Horn's persecution of the
"saints of the Highest One" {{ bible cite Dan 7:25 }} who rightly follow the Law of Moses. If, then, the Little Horn
is intent upon changing the Law of Moses, then they must be an entity who must have control of Jerusalem, since
exerting control over the Second Temple is a prerequisite to exerting control over the Law of Moses. The attempt of the
Little Horn to make a "change in seasons"{{ bible cite Dan 7:25 }} should not be understood as an attempt to
change how time is measured or change the yearly weather, but it instead should be understood as an attempt to change
*fate*, or the *appointed* time. Considering that the Little Horn's war against the saints is taking place shortly
before the Coming of the Son of Man, the Little Horn's rage against the seasons and the law can be understood
as an attempt to thwart the End of the Age. Each of these features of the Little Horn point to it being identified
*as* Jerusalem once more, just like the Clay in Daniel 2. That no singular "king" can be identified for Jerusalem
unlike the ten horns of the Fourth Beast can be explained away by the note made in {{ bible hover Dan 7:24 }} that
the Little Horn is "different" from the ten horns, and its greater appearance, combined with the horn imagery of the
Old and New Testament referring to power and authority in general, leads us to the conclusion that the Little Horn
is *more* than just a singular king. As for the three kings that fall before the Little Horn, there is a period
of time known as the Year of the Four Emperors in Roman history where three weak Caesars (Galba, Otho, and Vitellius)
ruled Rome after the assassination of Nero in 68 AD, with each of them having very short reigns during the civil war
that ensued. These three weak Caesars could also correlate to the toes of clay in {{ bible hover Dan 2:42 }}.
When Vespasian withdrew his army from Judea to end the civil war, there was a period of time when the
Jewish forces believed they had won the war against Rome, which could explain the Little Horn's boasting if it is
to be identified as Jerusalem. While there remain other data-points in Daniel 7 that could further
strengthen the case, we conclude that the Little Horn of Daniel 7 should be identified as Jerusalem and its authorities.

###{{ label rev-13 }} Revelation 13

{{ bible block Rev 13:1-3 }}

{{ bible block Rev 13:11-17 }}

Featuring the Beast of the Sea and the Beast of the Earth, this pair can also be mapped unto the prime suspects of
the Roman Empire and Jerusalem. As for the Beast of the Sea, it's description is directly drawn from Daniel 7, where
features from all four Beasts representing the four kingdoms that also come out of the sea in {{ bible hover Dan 7:2-7 }}.
Consequently, this Beast of the Sea can be understood as representing the fourth and final kingdom before the
Coming of the Son of Man that has subsumed the prior three kingdoms. This accords with history, in that the
Roman Empire was the most powerful empire in history up until that point and had adopted much of the preceding
kingdoms cultures and strategies.
The Beast of the Sea is also granted authority to war with the saints and overcome them for three and a half years
{{ bible cite Rev 13:5, 7 }}.
This too accords history, where Nero great persecution of the Church lasted three and a half years from the end of
64 AD up until Nero's death in the middle of 68 AD.
As for the seven heads of the Beast of the Sea, and the head that had been healed of
a fatal wound, more will unpacked in Revelation 17 that further connects the Beast of the Sea with the Roman Empire.
Another piece of evidence for the Beast of the Sea being an entity contemporaneous with the author and immediate audience
of the Book of Revelation in the first century AD is the author's warning in {{ bible hover Rev 13:9-10 }} that those
saints who received the book should be prepared to be slain by the Beast of the Sea, and that they should
persevere. This warning, in combinations with other warnings like the one found in {{ bible hover Rev 1:3 }}, point
to the Beast of the Sea being an entity that was a threat to the immediate audience of the Book of Revelation in the
first century.

The Beast of the Earth's identity can be more easily drawn from this passage however. The first piece of information
given that's relevant to its identity is that the Beast is seen "coming up out of the earth"{{ bible cite Rev 13:11 }}.
Once more, the Greek term for "earth" - γῆς (gēs) - should be understood as referring to *land*. In isolation this
could be understood still as referring to the whole world, but the details of the Beast of the Earth given in the
same verse limits the term's usage here down to *the* land, the Promised Land. The detail is that the Beast
"had two horns like a lamb" and spoke "as a dragon" {{ bible cite Rev 13:11 }}. The imagery of a lamb is a strong
allusion to the sacrificial system of the Law of Moses, and for the Beast to present with the horns of a lamb
suggests that the Beast has authority *over* the lambs, or over the sacrificial system. This can only be understood
as a reference to the priestly authority of Jerusalem and the Second Temple. That the same Beast also had the voice of a
dragon, with the dragon being later identified as being Satan himself {{ bible cite Rev 20:2 }}, suggests that it
reflects a perverse and unholy priestly system that deceives others regarding its purity, which strongly accords
with the state of Jerusalem's priestly authorities during Christ's earthly ministry {{ bible cite Mat 23:27-28 }}.
Consequently, the land the Beast arises out of should be understood as a reference to Judea.
The Beast of the Earth is then said to exercise "all the authority of the first beast in his presence". On the
assumption that the first beast, the Beast of the Sea, is to be identified with the Roman Empire, then the entity
that the Beast of the Earth is identified with must have exercised the authority of Rome with its permission. Jerusalem
once more matches this description{{ bible cite John 11:47-48 }}, with an example being that it was only able to deal
out capital punishment with the permission of Rome{{ cite antiquities[book:20, verse:200] }}.
As for the Beast of the Earth making "the earth and those
who dwell in it to worship the first beast, whose fatal wound was healed"{{ bible cite Rev 13:12 }}, it must be
demonstrated that the authorities of Jerusalem were actively leading Judea into an idolatrous relationship with Rome.
This can be demonstrated in multiple places in the New Testament, with {{ bible hover John 11:47-80 }} demonstrating
the priestly authorities willingness to kill Jesus to preserve their authority granted to them by Rome, and {{ bible
hover John 19:15 }} demonstrating Jerusalem's explicit idolatry with the masses declaring that they have
"no king but Caesar" in place of God {{ bible cite 1 Sam 8:7-8 }}.

When it states that the Beast of the Earth "does
great signs, so that he even makes fire come down out of heaven to the earth in the presence of men", it is not
necessary for there to be a literal manifestation of this sign in history. Instead, the imagery of fire coming down
out of heaven is meant to evoke Elijah's instances of the same miracle in the Old Testament
{{ bible cite 1 King 18:36-38, 2 King 1:10 }}, where it demonstrated the favour of God upon Elijah. Consequently,
the Beast of the Sea being able to perform the same miracle, even though it is idolatrous, is meant to symbolise
that the Beast of the Sea successfully deceives others that it has gained the favour of God. This can be seen in the
history of Jerusalem, since by being able to crucify Jesus, persecute His followers, grow and prosper,
and even for a time drive out Romans during the Jewish War, it appeared to both Jews and professing Christians
that were tempted to revert back to Judaism that Jerusalem was in fact favoured by God. {{ bible hover Rev 13:14 }}
specifically notes that it was God who gave the signs for the Beast of the Sea to do, for the purpose of raising
him before bring him down even lower.
This accords with Paul's explanation for God partially hardening Israel "until the fullness
of the Gentiles has come in"{{ bible cite Rom 11:25 }}, and that they are raised up as enemies of the saints for the
sake of the Gospel{{ bible cite Rom 11:28 }}. Consequently, the sign of fire being brought down out of heaven can be
attributed to Jerusalem in a non-literal manifestation.

As a result of deceiving the people of the land with this sign, the Beast of the Sea then commands them to make an image
of the Beast of the Sea {{ bible cite Rev 13:14 }}. The Beast of the Earth then gives life to the Image of the Beast,
granting the Image the ability to speak and causing those who do not worship the Image to be killed {{ bible cite
Rev 13:15 }}. The Beast of the Earth, pretending to be favoured by Yahweh, must be understood as presenting the
Image of the Beast as an image of Yahweh, much like Aaron presenting the Golden Calf
{{ bible cite Exodus 32:4-5 }}. That the Image is made in the likeness of the Beast of the Sea, yet is proclaimed as
God and takes on a life of its own, suggests that the forging of the Image of the Beast marks an act of rebellion
*from* the Beast of the Earth *against* the Beast of the Sea, since the people's worship is now redirected from the
Beast of the Sea to the now living Image of the Beast. It also parallels how the Little Horn of Daniel 7 took on a life
of its own and turned on the Fourth Beast {{ bible cite Dan 7:8 }}. This too would accord with the
history of Jerusalem, since even though the authorities of Jerusalem had long depended upon Rome as the foundation of
their power, the Jewish War began due to Jerusalem declaring they had no need for a Roman foundation anymore and that
they had the favour of God to conquer the Romans. However, the tyranny that the authorities of Jerusalem inflicted
upon Judea were worse than the tyranny of the Romans {{ cite jewish-war[book: 5, verse: 442] }},
all the while claiming the favour of God to ultimately win the war much like how Rome had come back from the brink
of death {{ cite jewish-war[book: 6, verse: 286] }}, and enforcing total submission
to the Second Temple system by the sword {{ cite antiquities[book: 20, verse: 200] }}. This supposed devotion to
Yahweh therefore was not only similar to their prior devotion to Caesar, but was even more perverse.

In regards to the Mark of the Beast in {{ bible hover Rev 13:16 }}, this is meant to be a deliberate perversion of
God's command in {{ bible hover Deut 6:8 }} and {{ bible hover Deut 11:18 }} to symbolically mark their forehead and
hands with the word of God, which represents their active devotion to God. The idolatrous repurposing of this imagery
from the Law of Moses reinforces the conclusion that the Image of the Beast is meant to be a deliberate mockery of
Yahweh worship. That those without the Mark are prevented from buying or selling accords with New Testament history
for how the saints of Jerusalem were often suffering great poverty {{bible cite Acts 4:34-35, 1 Cor 16:3, Rom 15:25-27}},
and they were excluded to a greater extent within Jerusalem as time went on leading up to the Jewish War. As for the
name and number of the Beast that the Mark consists of, the Beast in question could be either the Beast of the Earth
or the Beast of the Sea that Image depicts, since it is possible that it is the Image of the Beast that is acting in this
verse {{ bible cite Rev 13:16 }}.
This is where we admit there is still an element of mystery regarding candidate names for the number of the Beast,
six hundred and sixty-six. We do insist however that it is not necessary for it to be the name of an individual, since
the statement that "it is the number of man" in {{ bible hover Rev 13:18 }} is similar to the statement in
{{ bible hover Rev 21:17 }} where the point is that the measurements are human *comprehendible*, and are not impossible
to calculate. The candidate for the
name also needs to be knowable for the Greek-speaking recipients of the Book of Revelation in the 1st century, due to
the author's call for "him who has understanding" to calculate the number of the beast. The strongest contender for
the name
we believe was recorded by Iranaeus in the second century AD, where he posited the name Lateinos (ΛΑΤΕΙΝΟΣ), the
Greek name for the Romans. If this is true, then number of
the Beast could be a device used by the author of the Book of Revelation to help the audience recognise that the Roman
Empire, the *Latins*, are the Fourth Beast of Daniel {{ bible cite Dan 7:7 }}, and that even Jerusalem leading up to
Jewish War was to be identified with the Fourth Beast before splitting off as the Little Horn {{ bible cite Dan 7:8 }}.

###{{ label rev-17 }} Revelation 17 & 18

{{ bible block Rev 17:1-18 }}

Here the pairing of God's arch-enemies appear once more. Here, the author of Revelation is shown the impending
judgement of the Great Harlot {{ bible cite Rev 17:1 }}. The Great Harlot is seen seated upon a Scarlett Beast,
who once more has seven horns and ten horns {{ bible cite Rev 17:3 }}. The Great Harlot is stated as being clothed
in purple and scarlett, and adorned with gold, precious stones and pearls {{ bible cite Rev 17:4 }}. This imagery
immediately evokes the description of the garments of the high priest in {{ bible hover Exo 28 }}, hinting
that the Great Harlot is to be identified with the priestly authorities of Jerusalem. Yet, the Great Harlot as named
Babylon the Great according to what is written on her forehead {{ bible cite Rev 17:5 }}. Babylon is the greatest
archetypal enemy of God in the Old Testament, from the defiance of God's decree at Babel in {{ bible hover Gen 11 }}
through to King Nebuchadnezzar destroying Solomon's Temple in {{ bible hover 2 Kings 25 }}. The Great Harlot however
also has another title on her forehead, "Mother of harlots and of the abominations of the earth". The imagery of both
a harlot and Babylon in combination appears in {{ bible hover Eze 23 }}, where Jerusalem is depicted as whoring herself
out to Babylon in graphic detail {{ bible cite Eze 23:16-21 }}. Consequently, the Great Harlot is immediately associated
with both Jerusalem *and* Babylon simultaneously.

Then comes details regarding the Scarlett Beast, which are more challenging to unpack.
The seven heads and ten horns of the Scarlett Beast clearly identifies
it with both the Beast of the Sea in {{ bible hover Rev 13 }} and the Fourth Beast of {{ bible hover Dan 7 }}.
It's introduction as the Beast that "was, and is not, and is about to come up out of the abyss and go to destruction"
{{ bible cite Rev 17:8 }} is a deliberate inversion of Christ's title as the one "who is and who was and who is to come"
{{ bible cite Rev 1:8 }}. The imagery of the Scarlett Beast coming out of the abyss and then going to destruction
parallels Satan being released from the abyss at the end of the Millennium {{ bible hover Rev 20:3 }}. The Beast of the
Sea however, which the Scarlett Beast is equivalent, is destroyed at the beginning of the Millennium
{{ bible cite Rev 19:20 }}. The fate of the Scarlett Beast is therefore meant to deliberately mirror the later fate
of Satan. Alongside the statement that the Scarlett Beast "is not", there is another statement made that the Scarlett
Beast "is". The seven heads are explained as being seven mountains that the Great Harlot sits upon
{{ bible cite Rev 17:9 }}, which correlates strongly to the seven hills of Rome, the power that the Great Harlot
depends upon. The seven heads are also identified as seven kings, with five having already fallen, one currently
ruling, and another in the future set to reign for only short time from the point of view of the author of the Book of
Revelation {{ bible cite Rev 17:10 }}. When placed in the history of the Roman Empire, the Book of Revelation must have
been written during the reign of Nero, the sixth emperor of Rome, whose successor Galba reigned only for approximately
six months. Since Nero must have been alive during the writing of the Book of Revelation, his assassination could not
be the reference for the Scarlett Beast being *presently* regarded as "is not". Considering that the Scarlett Beast
is stated as being presently *in* the abyss {{ bible cite Rev 17:8 }}, and that the Great Harlot is also seated
upon many waters {{ bible cite Rev 17:1 }}, it appears that the abyss is to be equated with the many waters, and that
the Scarlett Beast is not only *in* the abyss, but *is* the abyss that the Great Harlot is seated upon. This accords
too with the Beast in {{ bible hover Rev 13 }} that is identified as Rome being described as "coming up out of the sea"
{{ bible cite Rev 13:1 }}. Later in {{ bible hover Rev 17:15 }} the many waters, and consequently the abyss, are also
equated with "peoples and crowds and nations and tongues". Consequently, the Scarlett Beast being "not" and then
arising out of the abyss can be understood as representing the cycle and sequence of the beastly kingdoms and
Satanic dominions of Daniel 7
rising ("was"), falling ("is not"), and rising again ("is about to come") repeatedly within the world ("the waters"),
ultimately manifesting in its latest form as the Roman Empire.

The Scarlett Beast itself is regarded as "an eighth and is one of the seven" kings
{{ bible cite Rev 17:11 }}. This mysterious phrase should be understood in light of the presently reigning king,
and that the full power of the Scarlett Beast is being wielded by the present king uniquely, "who goes to destruction".
The ten horns are also explained as being ten *different* kings from the kings the seven heads represent. These
ten "have not yet received a kingdom", but they will "receive authority as kings with the beast for one hour" for
one singular purpose that they lend their power and authority to the Scarlett Beast for {{ bible cite Rev 17:12-13 }}.
That singular purpose is to "wage war against the Lamb" {{ bible cite Rev 17:14 }},
which is what will last for only an hour before their defeat, symbolising a
limited period of time. Considering the prevalence and consistency of the references to the specific timespan of 42
months, or three and a half years, found across the Book of Revelation {{ bible cite Rev 11:2, Rev 13:5 }} and Daniel
{{ bible cite Dan 7:25, Dan 12:7 }}, the hour mentioned in {{ bible hover Rev 17:12 }} most likely refers to this
same finite period of time. The ten kings the horns represent must be contemporaneous with either the present
sixth or later seventh king of the Scarlett Beast's seven heads. If they are meant to be kings that have never possessed
a kingdom beforehand, then it appears that {{ bible hover Rev 17:20 }} is emphasising that these are entities that
are *made* kings by the Scarlett Beast granting them authority equivalent to that of a king, namely in the form of
waging war or executing capital punishment. The number of the kings, ten, could either represent another ten literal
individuals in history, or it could represent rather completeness in the form of every principality in the Scarlett
Beast being vested with the authority to wage war against the Lamb. This war against the Lamb must take place
before the Great Harlot is destroyed by the Scarlett Beast and the ten kings, as seen in {{ bible hover Rev 17:17 }}.
Assuming that the Great Harlot is Jerusalem, which will be further demonstrated in {{ bible hover Rev 18 }}, then
this war against the Lamb must have occurred between the reign of Nero and 70 AD. The candidate for this war against the
Lamb is therefore the persecution of the Church under Nero, which began at the end of 64 AD, and only ended with
Nero's assassination in the middle of 68 AD. This intense period of persecution, the first of its kind which was
empire-wide and resulted in the deaths of Apostles such as Paul and Peter, also lasted only three and a half years,
which accords with the likely meaning of the single hour in {{ bible hover Rev 17:12 }}. Due to the empire-wide nature
of the persecution, we therefore believe that the ten kings represent the principalities from *every* part of the Roman
Empire being granted the authority by the emperor to execute capital punishment on Christians. Upon the commencement
of the Jewish War and the death of Nero however, these principalities across the Roman Empire not only lent their
forces to destroy Jerusalem, but also began persecuting Jews found throughout the empire.
This aspect of history can explain the Scarlett Beast's and the
ten king's role in turning on and destroying the Great Harlot in {{ bible hover Rev 17:16-17 }}.

The last important statement made in Revelation 17 regarding the identity of the Great Harlot is found in
{{ bible hover Rev 17:18 }}. The Great Harlot is identified as the great city, "which has a kingdom over the kings of
the earth" {{ bible cite Rev 17:18 }}. The present tense of the statement signifies that the Great Harlot is to be
identified as a kingdom contemporaneous with the author of the Book of Revelation, which rules out a literal or
future Babylon. The reference to the "kings of the
earth" can once more be understood as referring to the kings of the *land*, which keeps Jerusalem and its authorities
as being candidates for the Great Harlot. The identification of the Great Harlot as Jerusalem however is confirmed
by her being name "the great city", since it is the same name given to Jerusalem earlier in
{{ bible hover Rev 11:8 }}. The fact that it states that this great city "has a kingdom over the kings of the earth"
{{ bible cite Rev 17:18 }} does not conflict with this identification, since there are two possible solutions for it.
The first involves taking the meaning of the greek word for "earth", γῆς (gēs), once more as referring to the
Promised Land. The "kings of the earth" would then be understood as the rulers of Judea, but this is rendered unlikely
by the details given for these kings in Revelation 18. The second option however
affirms that "earth" here has a wider scope beyond Judea if the dominion of the Great Harlot can
be equated with the dominion of Satan. Prior to the Coming of the Son of Man, Satan has dominion over the kingdoms
of the world {{ bible cite Matt 4:8-9 }}, and at the same time the Great Harlot is clearly Satanically influenced
{{ bible cite Rev 17:6 }}. Since the Great Harlot would be considered part of Satan's dominion, and since the depravity
of the Great Harlot is especially unique {{ bible cite Rev 11:8, 17:5 }}, the Great Harlot could be understood as the
focal point of Satan's dominion and his war against the Lamb, and consequently the kings of the *world* would be
subordinate to the Great Harlot. The Great Harlot can therefore still remain identified as Jerusalem.

{{ bible block Rev 18:2-3 }}

Here the kings of the earth are paralleled with "the nations", with the nations having the received the *wrath* of her
sexual immorality, whereas the kings have *commited* sexual immorality with her {{ bible cite Rev 18:3 }}.
This distinction in the English is less clear in the Greek, so therefore we will assume that both the nations *and* the
kings of the earth are deemed to have shared in the idolatry of the Great Harlot. The "merchants of the earth" are also
described as having become wealthy by through her {{ bible cite Rev 18:3 }}, where "earth" clearly refers to the same
"earth" that the kings are of. The reference to the nations in the same verse confirms that the scope of the "earth"
is beyond just that of the Judean territory. This by no means contradicts the identification of Jerusalem as the Great
Harlot, since the event of the Coming of the Son of Man was concerned both ending the deception of the nations by Satan
{{ bible cite Matt 24:29 }},
and of executing judgement against the apostates among God's people {{ bible cite Matt 24:15-16 }}. As mentioned before,
Satan's power of Israel, to the point of crucifying the Lord of glory, necessarily the dominion of Satan and the Beastly
Kingdom includes Jerusalem. Jerusalem was meant to be a light to the nations {{ bible cite Isa 49:6 }}, and Jerusalem
was an incredibly powerful and wealthy at the crossroads of the Roman Empire. That Jerusalem had fallen into apostasy,
and had thus borne God's name into falsehood before the whole known world at that time, means that the sins of Jerusalem
*did* have a "universal" scope that led others into sin and idolatry, Jew and Gentile alike.

An example of judgement language used by God which equates the judgement of the Jerusalem and the Near East
with a judgement of the whole "earth" is found in Jeremiah 25:

{{ bible block Jer 25:28-29 }}

{{ ! Reintegrate this argumentation back into the prior "earth" discussions }}

Those who are being offered the cup of the Lord's wrath listed prior in {{ bible hover Jer 25:17-26 }} that are
referred to as the "earth" span from Egypt through to Mesopotamia. While this judgement manifested in history through
the onslaught of the Neo-Babylonian Empire and the Empire's eventual subjugation by the Medo-Persian Empire, this
singular example demonstrates that references to the "remotest parts of the earth"{{ bible cite Jer 25:32 }},
"all the inhabitants of the earth"{{ bible cite Jer 25:29 }}, "the nations"{{ bible cite Jer 25:31 }},
can be understood as being local, even being strictly local to the Near East. Consequently, the use of "earth" and
"all the nations"{{ bible cite Rev 18:3 }} in Revelation 18 can therefore be understood as being local to the focus
of Revelation 18, which in this case are those who partake in and witness the destruction of the Great Harlot who
can be strongly identified as Jerusalem.

{{ bible block Rev 18:9-11 }}

{{ bible block Rev 18:15-19 }}

These details also accord with history regarding the destruction of Jerusalem, with the annihilation of one of the
wealthiest, strongest, and ancient cities in the world being a cataclysmic event that reverberated throughout the
whole Roman Empire.

{{ bible block Rev 18:24 }}

This is the last portion relevant to the identity of the Great Harlot in Revelation 18. The "all" refers to the
category of the prophets and saints, the faithful people of God, who have been slain. With "earth" being
defined as including and exceeding confines of Judea earlier, the bloodguilt for all slain saints is being attributed
to the Great Harlot. Once more the Great Harlot can be identified as Jerusalem through this, since Jerusalem not only
crucified Jesus, but exhorted the Roman authorities to persecute Christians across the empire, and applauded them
for doing so. Consequently, it is justified for the "blood" of the saints to be thought of as being found "in"
Jerusalem, due to it being the origin of their persecution.

### The Identity of the Fourth Beast and the Little Horn, and the timing of the First Resurrection

As a result of these studies across Daniel and Revelation, the recurring motif of a pair of entities that wage war
against God and His saints up until the Coming of the Son of Man can be strongly identified as two principalities
of Rome and Jerusalem. These principalities represented the focal points of Satan's dominion during the Apostolic age,
and consequently they together represent the fourth and final Beastly kingdom. If the Coming of the Son of Man occurred
in 70 AD, then the overthrow of the Fourth Beast and the Little Horn that make up the final
dominion of Satan would correspond with the end of the line of Caesars at the assassination of the first great persecutor
Nero, and with the Destruction of Jerusalem and the annihilation of the Jewish nation. {{ bible hover Dan 7:12 }}
also states that the three beasts that preceded the Fourth Beast have their dominions taken away, but they are granted
an extension of life "for an appointed season of time" *beyond* the Coming of the Son of Man in
{{ bible hover Dan 7:13 }}. While the preceding beasts are identified as the historical kingdoms of the Neo-Babylonian,
Medo-Persian, and Greek empires, the stripping of the dominions from these beasts means that we shouldn't understand
these surviving beasts as geographic empires, but that vestiges and rebellion of prior wordly kingdoms would be allowed
to persist during the reign of the Messiah. Importantly, the Beast of the Sea in {{ bible hover Rev 13:1-2 }} is
depicted as the convergence of each of the four Beasts of Daniel 7, so based off {{ bible hover Dan 7:12 }} an aspect
of the Beast of the Sea would have survived its defeat. Consequently, if the defeat of Fourth Beast and the Beast of
the Sea is to be identified with the end of the Caesars in 68 AD, then the survival of the Roman Empire out of the
subsequent civil war and the victory of Vespasian can be understood as the manifestation of the lesser beastly powers
that were permitted to endure beyond the Coming of the Son of Man, since the Roman Empire never regained the glory
and divine status of the Pax Romana under the rule of the Caesars.

Now that we have identified confidently the Fourth Beast and the Little Horn, we can return to the matter of the
First Resurrection. The defeat of the Fourth Beast and the Little Horn and the chaining of Satan mark the inauguration
of the Millennium {{ bible cite Rev 20:2 }}, and the inauguration of the Millennium in turn also marks the
First Resurrection {{ bible cite Rev 20:4-6 }}. We therefore come to the matter of defining the First Resurrection.

The clearest passage regarding the First Resurrection is found in Revelation 20:

{{ bible block Rev 20:4-6 }}

The author of Revelation stating that he "saw thrones", and that "*they* sat on them", is a direct allusion to
Daniel 7:

{{ bible block Dan 7:9-10 }}

The thrones are set up before the Ancient of Days and prior to the Coming of the Son of Man in
{{ bible hover Dan 7:13 }}, and the heavenly court are the "they" in {{ bible hover Rev 20:4 }} who sit on the thrones
that are set up {{ bible cite Dan 7:10 }}. Once "they" are seated, and the books are opened, the Fourth Beast and
the Little Horn are *then* destroyed {{ bible cite Dan 7:11 }}. When the thrones are set up in {{ bible hover Rev 20:4 }}
the Beast and the False Prophet have already been destroyed in {{ bible hover Rev 19:20 }}, so this scene that the
author of Revelation "saw" in {{ bible hover Rev 20:4 }} is another view of this moment in Revelation 19.
This scene involves Christ vindicating the martyrs who endured the Beast and the False Prophet by granting them the
First Resurrection. This occurs at the Coming of the Son of Man, based off {{ bible hover 1 Cor 15:23 }}. This also
accords with the sequence of events in {{ bible hover Dan 7:12-13 }} with the Coming of the Son of Man occurring
immediately after the thrones are set up for judgement against the Fourth Beast and the Little Horn.
This First Resurrection is identical in quality with Christ's Resurrection, that being a physical and immortal
resurrection {{ bible cite 1 Cor 15:23, Rev 20:4 }}. Those who receive the First Resurrection then reign with
Christ throughout the Millennium {{ bible cite Rev 20:6 }}. Those who receive the First Resurrection however are
*only* those martyred or resisted taking the Mark of the Beast while the Beast and the False Prophet were *still alive*.
Since the Beast and the False Prophet were destroyed by 70 AD, the recipients of the First Resurrection *must* have
at least been the Christians of the Apostolic era. How can it be said however that the Apostolic Christians received the First
Resurrection when their bones are still amongst us to this day? The bones of the Apostle Peter still lies beneath the
Vatican, for example. Our answer to this is that it is not necessary for the bodies of a Christian to be
physically raised out of the ground for the soul of the Christian to be resurrected with an immortal body. Christ's
resurrection was unique in how His crucified body was raised immortal, along with His wounds, and He retained that
immortal but wounded body when He ascended into heaven {{ bible cite Luke 24:51, Rev 5:6 }}. However, the promise
of immortal resurrection for the Christian is not that their wounds are retained {{ bible cite Rev 21:4, 22:3 }} -
the martyrs who were beheaded in {{ bible hover Rev 20:4 }} will not remain headless at their resurrection. Instead,
the Christian "puts on the incorruptible" and "puts on immortality" {{ bible cite 1 Cor 15:53 }}, and their
mortal body is likened to a seed that is "sown in weakness", and "raised in power" {{ bible cite 1 Cor 15:43 }}.
Importantly, {{ bible hover 1 Cor 15:44 }} states a "natural body" is sown, and "it is raised a spiritual body" of
a very different substance. Consequently, the mortal body of the Christian does not need to be integrated into the
immortal body their soul receives. With this understanding of Christian Resurrection applied to the First Resurrection,
if the First Resurrection occurred at the Coming of the Son of Man, then the souls of Apostolic Christians such as the
Apostle Peter could have received their physical resurrection *without* their mortal bodies having to leave the ground.
Where, then, does the First Resurrection takes place? The answer is also found in {{ bible hover Rev 20:4 }}. Just
as the direct reference to {{ bible hover Dan 7:9 }} has the thrones being set up *in heaven*, the souls of the
Apostolic Christians are also seen and are raised to life *in heaven*. Just as the physical man Christ Jesus stands
before the Father *in heaven* {{ bible cite 1 Tim 2:5 }}, so can His martyrs in physical form. Consequently, the
First Resurrection can be understood as taking place entirely in heaven.

##{{ label millennium }} The Millennium

{{ bible block 1 Cor 15:20-26 }}

To argue that the Coming of the Son of Man occurred at the Destruction of Jerusalem in 70 AD, we also have to argue
that the Millennium also began in 70 AD. First of all, we must define the Millennium based off the clearest passages
that refer to it.

{{ bible block Rev 20:1-3 }}

Aside from the First Resurrection, the beginning of the Millennium is identified with the binding of Satan
{{ bible cite Rev 20:2 }}. Satan is thrown and sealed within the Abyss "so that he would not deceive the nations any
longer, until the thousand years were finished" {{ bible cite Rev 20:3 }}. The Abyss has previously been identified
in {{ bible hover Rev 17:8,15 }} as the nations that revolt against God, and this understanding can apply here due to
the purpose of Satan's binding is prevent him from deceiving the nations, which represents only a partial limitation
of his power. Satan's prior deceit of the nations before this point is meant to be contrasted with the
advance of the Gospel and the Kingdom of Heaven {{ bible cite Matt 24:14 }}, and he is bound so that it advance of the
Gospel is successful. It can be inferred from this passage however that even though he may be powerless before
the advance of the Gospel during the Millennium, Satan himself is not powerless within the Abyss that consist of the
Children of Wrath. The Apostle Peter's comparison of Satan to a prowling and devouring lion in
{{ bible hover 1 Pet 5:8 }} for example would still be applicable of him within the Abyss during the Millennium,
with Satan still being capable of sowing chaos amongst the nations as seen in this present day.

The only other detail given in Revelation 20 regarding the nature of the Millennium are those related to the receivers
of the First Resurrection reigning with Christ and ministering as priests before Him throughout the entire Millennium
{{ bible cite Rev 20:6 }}. While the nature of this reign and ministry of First Resurrection Christians during the
Millennium is mysterious, but the nature of Christ's reign during the Millennium is clear:

{{ bible block Psa 2:1-12 }}

{{ bible block Psa 110:1-7 }}

What each of these central Messianic psalms make clear is that Christ's reign is in the midst of actively rebellious
nations who yet fall within His possession {{ bible cite Psa 2:8, 110:2 }}. These details of the Messianic reign also
further reinforce that the Coming of the Son of Man is not meant to mark the end of history but the beginning of process
of all the nations yielding to Him.

{{ bible block 1 Cor 15:23-28 }}

Paul builds upon {{ bible hover Psalm 110:1 }} by stating that *after* the Coming of the Son of Man and the First
Resurrection {{ bible cite 1 Cor 15:23 }}, this process of abolishing the powers and authorities of the world begins
until the last enemy Death is abolished {{ bible cite 1 Cor 15:25-26 }}, which would mark the Second Resurrection
immediately after the end of the Millennium {{ bible cite Rev 21:4 }}.

The centre-point of the Messiah's reign in the Psalms is also identified as Zion {{ bible cite Psa 2:6, 110:2 }}, which
in the New Testament context is to be understood as the Heavenly Jerusalem {{ bible cite Mat 28:18, Rev 21:10 }}.
Christ therefore rules over the nations from heaven during the Millennium, and the centre of Christ's reign only
transfers to Earth after the Millennium and the Second Resurrection, as seen in {{ bible hover Rev 21:1-4 }}.

Interestingly, in {{ bible hover Rev 21:1 }} there is a reference to the creation of a new heavens and a new earth that
is drawn from {{ bible hover Isa 65:17 }}, but these new heavens and new earths are very different between Revelation
and Isaiah, the most significant of them being that death is present in Isaiah's version {{ bible cite Isa 65:20 }},
whereas it is abolished in Revelation's version {{ bible cite Rev 21:4 }}. These should therefore be understood as two
different sets of new heavens and earth, which accords with there being two ages promised in Revelation of the
Millennium *before* the Second Resurrection and the Eternity *after* the Second Resurrection. The relevant passage
from Isaiah 65 that would therefore correspond with the Millennium is found below:

{{ bible block Isa 65:17-24 }}

If we to understand ourselves as Christians who are living *in* the Millennium, then this vision of the Kingdom of
Heaven may appear Utopian and distant. But when understood in light of Christ's parables of the Kingdom of Heaven
being comparable to a mustard seed and leaven, Isaiah's vision can be understood as the *full* manifestation of the
Kingdom of Heaven over time by the end of the Millennium. Even as Christians of the present day, all can attest that
the advancement of Christianity up until this point in history has resulted in an unmatched level of prosperity,
life and justice, even if there have been active and successful attempts to undo that progress in recent times. Isaiah
65 should therefore be understood as a glimpse of the world by the end of the Millennium, prior to the releasing of
Satan:

{{ bible block Rev 20:7-10 }}

Satan at the end of the Millennium is released by God to deceive the nations again like he was capable of prior to the
Millennium. The nations that he deceives are drawn from the farthest parts of the earth and are equated with Gog and
Magog from Ezekiel 38 and 39, and the details given for how they are gathered for war, are innumerable in number,
march against Jerusalem, and are finally destroyed by a supernatural act of God {{ bible cite Rev 20:8-9 }} match
perfectly with the details given in Ezekiel. Relevant to the nature of the Millennium, Ezekiel 38 provides extra
details regarding the inhabitants of Jerusalem "in the last years" who are gathered out of "the peoples" after
the land of Israel had been for the longest time a "continual waste", and were all now "living securely" after having
been "restored from the sword"
{{ bible cite Eze 38:8 }}, dwelling "quietly" in villages without bars, walls, or gates {{ bible cite Eze 38:11 }}.
This description of the inhabitants of Jerusalem accords perfectly with the description of the same inhabitants
in {{ bible hover Isa 65:18-19 }} by the end of the Millennium. In regards to whether the Jerusalem being
referred to in Ezekiel 38, Isaiah 65, and Revelation 20 represents the Church or a restored earthly Jerusalem, Romans 11
opens the door to an earthly Jerusalem and the Promised Land being restored *after* the "fullness of Gentiles has come
in" and "all Israel" are saved by the Gospel {{ bible cite Rom 11:25-26 }}. We do not believe the present day
State of Israel is
the fulfillment of this restored earthly Jerusalem and a Promised Land, since neither of the conditionals laid out
in Romans 11 have been accomplished, but by the end of the Millennium it could be a reality that is central to the
release of Satan and its aftermath.

The final detail provided in Revelation 20 regarding the end of the Millennium is the destruction of Satan after the
annihilation of Gog and Magog {{ bible cite Rev 20:10 }}. Satan joins the Beast and the False Prophet in the Lake of
Fire, which is a data-point is useful for reinforcing that the destruction of the Beast and the False Prophet in 70 AD
and the destruction of Satan bookend the Millennium. In all, the picture that is painted of the Millennium across these
primary texts accords strongly with the imagery in {{ bible hover Dan 2:34-35 }} of the Stone Cut Without Hands toppling
the Statue, representing the initial vindication and triumph of the Coming of the Son of Man, becoming then a "great
mountain" that "filled the whole earth", a process that reaches the final state of peace, prosperity, and true religion
depicted in Isaiah's new heavens and earth, before the final chapter of Satan's release commences. While the rate and
trajectory of progress between these start and end points of history is not defined, the state of these start and end
points are.

## The Second Resurrection

{{ bible block Rev 20:11-15 }}

The Second Resurrection immediately follows the destruction of Satan in {{ bible hover Rev 20:10 }}. All those who
did not receive the First Resurrection in {{ bible hover Rev 20:4 }} are physically resurrected at this point in time,
as according to {{ bible hover Rev 20:5 }}. In contrast with the First Resurrection, the Second Resurrection clearly
takes place on Earth as Christ and His throne descend and earth and heaven flee before Him {{ bible cite Rev 20:11 }}.
Those who are judged are receive eternal life or eternal damnation, according to whether their name is found written
in the Book of Life {{ bible cite Rev 20:15 }}. While we have up until this point been describing the Beast, False
Prophet, and Satan as being "destroyed" when thrown into the Lake of Fire, the persistence of the Beast and the
False Prophet within the Lake of Fire when Satan is thrown in suggests eternal torment rather than annihilation
{{ bible cite Rev 20:10 }}. The Second Resurrection would also be identical to the First Resurrection in terms of
granting an immortal resurrection to both the condemned and the redeemed, since the Second Resurrection is only
distinguished from the First Resurrection in {{ bible hover Rev 20:5 }} according to *when* it takes place and *who*
receives it. A parallel description of the Second Resurrection is found at the beginning of Daniel 12:

{{ bible block Dan 12:1-3 }}

An interesting detail of {{ bible hover Dan 12:2 }} is that it does not say *all* "who sleep in the dust of the ground
will awake", but "many". This distinction can be understood in light of the First Resurrection already having taken
place before the End of Days in Daniel 12. The "many" then similarly receive everlasting life or contempt after they
are judged. For one to undergo everlasting contempt after their resurrection, they necessarily need to receive
immortality, even though they are thrown into the Lake of Fire and undergo the Second Death {{ bible cite Rev 20:13 }}.
Consequently, the *quality* of resurrection both the condemned and redeemed received is the same.

The Second Resurrection then ends with Death and Hades being the last realms or curses to be thrown into the Lake of
Fire. This accords once more with the timeline provided by Paul in 1 Corinthians 15:24-28 when he says:

{{ bible block 1 Cor 15:24-28 }}

There is potentially another reference to the Second Resurrection in 1 Corinthians 15:50-57:

{{ bible block 1 Cor 15:50-57 }}

Paul specifically refers to the "last trumpet" in {{ bible hover 1 Cor 15:52 }}, which is a key part of the "mystery"
that Paul received {{ bible cite 1 Cor 15:51 }}. It being a "mystery" suggests that Paul received only partial revelation
concerning the End of the Age and the Age to Come.
Considering that the Book of Revelation was intended to be the final and clearest explanation
for what had been prior to it only partial revelations received by the Church concerning the Coming of the Son of Man
and beyond {{ bible cite 1 Cor 13:8-12 }}, we must interpret this "mystery" in light of the Book of Revelation.
Consequently, when Paul speaks about the "last trumpet" alongside the alive and dead putting on "immortality", then
we should understand this as him describing the Second Resurrection, since nowhere else is the
First Resurrection referred to as being the "last" action in history, and the "dead" and the alive here who are
resurrected encompasses all of humanity, as opposed to the First Resurrection being strictly applied to the dead in
Christ.

## The Rapture

These discussions of Paul in relation to the Second Resurrection cannot continue without dealing with key Rapture texts
such as 1 Thessalonians 4:13-18, whose common interpretation appears to contradict the timeline of the
First and Second Resurrection:

{{ bible block 1 Thessalonians 4:13-18 }}

Here Paul states that here he is conveying "the word of the Lord" {{ bible cite 1 Thess 4:15 }} directly, which could
be the same as the "mystery" revealed to him in {{ bible cite 1 Cor 15:51 }}, since both of these revelations are
concerned with resurrection. The First Resurrection is once more stated as occurring at the Coming of the Son of Man
when he says "the dead in Christ will rise first" {{ bible cite 1 Thess 4:15-16 }}, matching
{{ bible hover 1 Cor 15:23 }}. Paul then goes on to discuss what will happen to those who are alive and remaining
*after* the Coming of the Son of Man. Paul interestingly notes that *after* Christ descends from heaven {{ bible cite
1 Thess 4:16 }}, He still remains "in the air" {{ bible cite 1 Thess 4:17 }}, which reinforces the case that the
Coming of the Son of Man occurs entirely in heaven, with the descent mentioned by Paul being the Son of Man descending
on the "clouds of heaven" to receive the Kingdom from the Ancient of Days in {{ bible hover Dan 7:13 }}. In
{{ bible hover 1 Thess 4:14 }}, Paul also states that God will bring with Him "those who have have fallen asleep in
Jesus", which are the recipients of the First Resurrection, which means the First Resurrection has already occurred,
and therefore the descent of God being described must the Great White Throne scene of {{ bible hover Rev 20:11 }} that
corresponds with the Second Resurrection. Paul makes this distinction same between the Son and the Father in
{{ bible hover 1 Cor 15:23-24 }} when he discusses "Christ" handing over the kingdom "to the God and Father" at the
Second Resurrection.
Pauls primary point in this passage is that those who are alive will *not* precede the dead who receive the
First Resurrection at the Coming of the Son of Man. It would be a mistake to interpret this passage as stating that
those who are alive receive the resurrection immediately after those who receive the First Resurrection, since it
would not only contradict the timeline of the {{ bible hover Rev 20:5 }}, but it would diminish the significance of the
reward of the First Resurrection for the dead in Christ at His coming if those who are alive receive it immediately
after. Everything else that is described in {{ bible hover 1 Thess 4:17-18 }} must therefore pertain to either
Millennium or the Second Resurrection. Paul does not explicitly discuss the resurrection of those "who are alive
and remain" in this portion unlike his discussion of the Second Resurrection in {{ bible hover 1 Cor 15:50-57 }},
which leads to believe that it is the Millennium in view. The language of those remaining being "caught up" (raptured)
in {{ bible hover 1 Thess 4:17 }} has its only parallel found in the Olivet Discourse:

{{ bible block Mat 24:31 }}

The angels who go out to "gather" are sent out after the "great trumpet", which matches the rapture occurring after
the "trumpet of God". The gathering should therefore be equated to the rapture. However, we have already established
that {{ bible hover Mat 24:31 }} marks the very beginning of the Millennium, and that the gathering of the elect is
therefore the beginning of a process. Consequently, the rapture must also be referring to this same process within
the Millennium. Also, since there is no resurrection in view in {{ bible 1 Thess 4:17 }}, and that the designation of
those "who are alive and remain" refers to those live past the Coming of the Son of Man who are not themselves exempt
from death during the Millennium, then them meeting "the Lord in the air" as well as "them" who received the First
Resurrection should be understood as the *souls* of Christians being gathered to Christ in heaven over the course
of the Millennium leading up to the Second Resurrection, whereas prior to the Coming of the Son of Man all the
souls of the faithful were gathered with Abraham in Hades {{ bible cite Luke 16:23 }}.

The other key Rapture text is found in 2 Thessalonians 2:

{{ bible block 2 Thess 2:1-12 }}

In {{ bible hover 2 Thess 2:1 }} we have another reference to the "gathering" that follows the Coming of the Son of
Man, which confirms the link between the Rapture and the gathering in mentioned in {{ bible hover Mat 24:31 }}. As
for the other details provided for the signs leading up to the Coming of the Son of Man, particularly the Man of
Lawlessness {{ bible cite 2 Thess 2:3 }}, they can be strongly identified in history leading up to 70 AD and the
Destruction of Jerusalem. Firstly, "the apostasy" can be equally rendered "the falling away" or "the rebellion", which
can be understood simultaneously as the abandonment and betrayal of the faith by previously professing Christians
that Christ warned about in {{ bible hover Mat 24:9-13 }}, or as a political and armed rebellion. The likelihood of it
referring to a political rebellion is bolstered by the apostasy being associated with revealing of the Man of
Lawlessness. The contrast of this Man against lawfulness immediately brings the Law of Moses into view, and
subsequently, the rebellion of Judah, the First Jewish War beginning in 66 AD, as well. A key part of the Man of
Lawlessness' identity is also that he seats himself within the Second Temple, thereby exalting himself in his blasphemy
{{ bible cite 2 Thess 2:4 }}. In history there can be no doubt that the Man of Lawlessness can be identified as
John of Gischala, the false Messiah who led and tyrannized Jerusalem during the First Jewish War, who induced
Jerusalem's false prophets to proclaim their ultimate deliverance by God and victory over the Romans
{{ cite jewish-war[book: 6, verse: 286] }}, and who
made his headquarters and his seat *within* the Second Temple until Jerusalem was overthrown by Titus in
70 AD. Paul, writing 2 Thessalonians in the early 50's AD, states that the Thessalonian church "know what restrains
him now, so that in his time he will be revealed" {{ bible cite 2 Thess 2:6 }}, meaning that this Restrainer which
held back the Man of Lawlessness was no mystery for them. This Restrainer is then stated as holding back "lawlessness"
only until the Restrainer itself is taken out of the way {{ bible cite 2 Thess 2:7 }}, which could mean that it either
unleashes it willingly or is overwhelmed by it. In the context of what led up to the First Jewish War, this Restrainer
could be understood the remnant of the leadership in Judah that were zealous for the Law but did everything in their
power to prevent Judah from rebelling against Rome and igniting a war they could never win. Flavius Josephus would then
be considered one of the restrainers by this definition, until the rebellion grew beyond his control and he willingly
joined it out of his love for Judah but against hope of victory. We therefore identify the Restrainer as those Paul
described in {{ bible hover Rom 10:1-3 }} as those "have a zeal for God, but not according to knowledge".
Lastly, the slaying of the Man of Lawlessness by the Coming of the Son of Man can be understood as John of Gischala and
his followers being captured and paraded in chains through streets of Rome by Titus where he died in captivity
{{ cite jewish-war[book: 7, verse: 3] }}. We therefore
conclude confidently from this text that the Rapture should be understood as the gathering of God's elect "from the
four winds" {{ bible hover Mat 24:31 }} that *commenced* following the Destruction of Jerusalem and the
Coming of the Son of Man in 70 AD.

# Conclusion

{{ bible block Rev 21:1-4 }}

In conclusion, approaching Christ's words in {{ bible hover Mat 24:34 }} in combination with the Book of Revelation as
if they were intended to be sources of clarity instead of confusion results in coherent timeline for the End of the Age
and the Age to Come. The timeline that has been presented in this study ends up according closely with the common form
of Postmillennialism with some variations in regards to how the First Resurrection is understood, the identification
of the Beast of the Book of Revelation, and the identity of Man of Lawlessness. This proposed variant of
Postmillennialism insists upon the Coming of the Son of Man and the First Resurrection as occurring in 70 AD as the
fulfillment of Christ's prophecy in the Olivet Discourse, and that as present-day Christians living currently in the
Millennium we are part of the work of the King Jesus putting "all His enemies under His feet"
{{ bible cite 1 Cor 15:25 }} up until the day of the Second Resurrection and the death of Death. As for a name for
this proposed variant, Postmillennialism inherently causes confusion. Literally meaning "after the Millennium", it's
name in isolation sounds as if it is a position that defends that we are presently living *after* the Millennium to
mirror to how Premillennialism defends that we presently live *before* the Millennium. Even though Postmillennialism
actually defends that we *presently* live in the Millennium, the name is still explained as meaning that Second Coming
of Christ occurs at the end of the Millennium. This too causes confusion though, because it defends at the same time
that the Coming of the Son of Man occurred in 70 AD. The name of Amillennialism similarly causes confusion, since it
literally means "no Millennium", even though it too defends that we presently live in the Millennium. Since
Premillennialism is the only position with a name that unambiguously represents what it defends, we propose that the
new name *Intramillennialism* should be used to represent our position, literally meaning "within the Millennium".

We believe that defending Intramillennialism benefits not only apologetics in demonstrating the fulfillment of the Olivet
Discourse and the Book of Revelation precisely as Jesus prophesied, but also many other areas of Biblical theology.
One area that it is immediately relevant for is its ability to counter Charismatic theology that insists that the
Apostolic and Prophetic gifts are being poured out on the Church now that the End of the Age and the Coming of the Son
of Man is imminent. In response, not only does Intramillennialism
agree with Peter in {{ bible hover Acts 2:16 }} that the Prophet Joel's prophecy in {{ bible hover Joel 2:28-29 }} for
the End of the Age was fulfilled at Pentecost, but it is able to demonstrate from {{ bible hover 1 Cor 13:8-12 }}
that the Apostolic and Prophetic gifts ceased upon the Coming of the Son of Man
("the perfect" {{ bible cite 1 Cor 13:10 }}) after 70 AD, which accords with the surviving testimony of early Church
history from the second century onwards of there being no Apostolic and Prophetics gifts that matched what was
experienced during the Apostolic era. Intramillennialism, exactly like Postmillennialism, also provides a hope for the
future of this age that is not present in Pre or Amillennialism, with the clear optimistic endpoint of the Millennium
reinforcing the Calvinistic claim that God and His people will succeed their mission, regardless of the attempts of
man or spirit to thwart it. This too provides hope for Christians that are enduring and losing their lives to the
remaining evil in the world. While there remain key foundational assumptions that this argument for Intramillennialism
is heavily dependent on, with the dating and authorship of the Book of Revelation being particularly critical, this
will be defended elsewhere. We hope that Intramillennialism provides a consistent and coherent way of comprehending
the Bible, the End of the Age, the Age to Come, and our present day place in history.